WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:07.520
Hi. This is Matt Baker. Welcome to Episode 3 in&nbsp;
my series on Space Exploration. In Episode 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.200
we looked at the early space race between&nbsp;
the Soviet Union and the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:18.560
which led to the US landing the first human&nbsp;
on the moon in 1969. Then, in Episode 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:24.040
we looked at how, during the 70s and 80s, the&nbsp;
Soviet Union went on to build space stations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:30.600
whereas the US went on to build space shuttles;&nbsp;
but then, how—after the end of the Cold War—Russia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:35.440
and the US started to work together (along with&nbsp;
several other countries), eventually building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:41.080
the International Space Station, which could be&nbsp;
visited by both the Russian Soyuz spacecrafts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:46.000
as well as by the US space shuttles. In today’s&nbsp;
episode we are therefore going to start in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.640
year 2003 (during the early days of the ISS)&nbsp;
and continue all the way up to today, again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:59.280
focusing mostly on human spaceflight but this&nbsp;
time on what has been dubbed the “new” space race.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:04.200
Take note however that there will be a final&nbsp;
episode (Episode 4), in which I’ll switch gears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:10.400
and cover robotic missions instead, meaning the&nbsp;
ones sent to Venus, Mars, Jupiter, et cetera.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:15.320
Now, before I begin, I want to remind you that&nbsp;
you can get a copy of this chart as a poster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:21.600
over on our website UsefulCharts.com. It&nbsp;
covers every major space mission from 1957&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.640
to the missions planned for the 2030s and,&nbsp;
like all of our charts, it’s printed in high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:32.200
resolution on thick quality paper. Take note&nbsp;
that we also sell a Timeline of World History,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:38.040
a Timeline of US History, an Evolutionary&nbsp;
Tree of Life poster as well as many more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:41.640
Again, if you’re interested in learning&nbsp;
more about any of these wallcharts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:47.320
just head over to UsefulCharts.com by clicking&nbsp;
the link in the description or pinned comment.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:07.440
Okay, let’s now zoom in on the timeline,&nbsp;
to the year 2003, because that’s the year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.560
that two events occurred, which together,&nbsp;
set the stage for the new space race (the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:18.800
one that’s still going on today). One of these&nbsp;
events was the launch of Shenzhou 5 by China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:25.360
which successfully sent Yang Liwei into space for&nbsp;
4 days. You see, up to this point, there were only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:31.320
two countries in the world with the ability to&nbsp;
launch humans: Russia and the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:36.800
Sure, individuals from 30 other countries had also&nbsp;
travelled to space by this point but all of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:43.480
had done so by hitching a ride on either a Russian&nbsp;
or American spacecraft. Shenzhou5 was different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:49.600
because there was now a third country—China—that&nbsp;
had the ability to send humans into space on its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:55.200
own—a fact that would eventually become a game&nbsp;
changer. I’ll come back to China in a bit but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:01.200
first, I want to go back to 2003 and point out&nbsp;
the other big thing that happened that year.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.800
By January of 2003, the International Space&nbsp;
Station had been in operation for about 2 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:12.480
and had been occupied by six permanent crews,&nbsp;
consisting of both American and Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:18.320
astronauts. In other words: the two countries&nbsp;
were cooperating well and things were looking up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:25.440
But then, tragedy struck. Most people remember the&nbsp;
Challenger disaster (which happened in 1986) but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:31.280
many forget that there was actually a second&nbsp;
space shuttle disaster, in 2003, this time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:36.960
involving the Columbia (the official flight&nbsp;
number being STS-107). In this instance, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:42.560
tragedy occurred at the end of the mission, rather&nbsp;
than during launch, meaning that the astronauts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:48.440
actually spent 16 days in space but then never&nbsp;
made it home. Basically, while re-entering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:54.200
earth’s atmosphere, an undetected hole in the&nbsp;
shuttle’s heat shied caused the entire thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.200 --> 00:04:01.840
to disintegrate, killing all seven astronauts on&nbsp;
board. Now, while STS-107 was not an ISS mission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:08.200
the disaster did have a major impact on the ISS&nbsp;
because, well, the station wasn’t finished yet and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:14.280
it was the space shuttle that was meant to do most&nbsp;
of the remaining construction work. This left NASA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:20.880
with a dilemma. Of the 4 original shuttles they&nbsp;
had built, 2 had now met with disaster and while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:26.640
they now had a 5th one (meaning that they still&nbsp;
had 3), the program’s overall track record was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:32.400
not looking good. Perhaps it was time to scrap the&nbsp;
shuttles altogether. But that would mean leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:38.120
the International Space station unfinished. In the&nbsp;
end, the decision was made to use the remaining 3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:44.560
shuttles to finish the ISS but then to shut down&nbsp;
the shuttle program as soon as that was done.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:50.800
So, in 2005, shuttle flights resumed and over the&nbsp;
next six years, several more ISS components were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:57.320
added, including the US Harmony node, a European&nbsp;
science lab (called Columbus), a Japanese science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.320 --> 00:05:04.680
lab (called Kibo), and the Tranquility node—which&nbsp;
is where the cupola is located. That’s the famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:11.280
dome-shaped window you’ve probably seen in&nbsp;
photos. By 2011, the International Space station&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:17.680
was complete and therefore the US space shuttle&nbsp;
program officially came to an end, with the 135th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:23.720
and final flight taking place in July. This left&nbsp;
the US in an awkward position because, while it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:29.320
had initially started to develop a new spacecraft&nbsp;
to replace the shuttle, the program developing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:35.800
was cancelled in 2010 and replaced with a new&nbsp;
program—one that would rely entirely on private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:41.600
companies to develop new spacecrafts. But these&nbsp;
new commercial spacecrafts were far from ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:49.360
so for about a decade, the US had to rely entirely&nbsp;
on Russia to get its astronauts to the ISS.

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:56.520
Because of all of this, during the 2010s, a new&nbsp;
space race emerged—one that not only saw China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:01.680
competing against the rest of the world but also&nbsp;
one that saw several private companies competing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:07.080
with each other to become NASA’s primary partner.&nbsp;
But at this point, let me now go back and tell you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:12.520
a bit more about the Chinese space program. Like&nbsp;
I said, they passed the first major hurdle—putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:19.640
a human in space—in 2003, with Shenzhou 5. This&nbsp;
was followed by Shenzhou 6 (which put 2 people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:25.520
in space) and then Shenzhou 7 (which put 3&nbsp;
and included China’s first ever spacewalk).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:28.920
Now, at this point, I should mention&nbsp;
that each spacefaring country uses a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:34.920
slightly different term when referring to its&nbsp;
space travellers. The US uses “astronaut”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:43.280
Russia uses “cosmonaut” and China uses “taikonaut”&nbsp;
(taiko being the Chinese word for space). So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:49.120
the next major step for the taikonauts was&nbsp;
to perform a docking in space. To do this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:55.480
China built a small space station prototype called&nbsp;
Tiangong-1, which was visited for the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.480 --> 00:07:03.840
by Shenzhou 9 in 2012, and then by Shenzhou 10 the&nbsp;
following year. Shenzhou 9 is notable in that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:09.760
carried China’s first female taikonaut, Liu Yang.&nbsp;
China then launched a second prototype station,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:16.440
Tiangong-2, which was visited once. You see, all&nbsp;
of this was just preparation for China’s first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:23.640
actual space station, which was launched in 2021.&nbsp;
It’s simply called Tiangong (which, btw, means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:31.800
“heavenly palace”) Like the ISS, it is a modular&nbsp;
station. Currently, there are 3 modules: Tianhe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:37.920
Wentian, and Mengtian (although, eventually&nbsp;
it will have six). To date, the Tiangong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:45.160
space station has been visited 11 times and&nbsp;
has been permanently occupied since 2022.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:50.960
But, very soon, it will be visited for a 12th&nbsp;
time (and the new crew will include—for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:56.680
first time ever—a non-Chinese citizen:&nbsp;
one of these two men from Pakistan).

00:07:56.680 --> 00:08:03.080
Now, in case you’re wondering, here’s how Tiangong&nbsp;
compares to the ISS in terms of size. As you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:08.720
see, it’s quite a bit smaller. It’s also important&nbsp;
to note that the two space stations do not ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:14.320
communicate with each other. One of the reasons&nbsp;
for this is that the US passed a law in 2011&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:20.800
(known as the Wolf Amendment) which prohibits&nbsp;
NASA from sharing any kind of data with China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:25.760
for security reasons. So how then do&nbsp;
they avoid crashing into each other?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:30.960
Well, for one thing, they orbit at slightly&nbsp;
different angles. But more importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:36.120
each station constantly monitors potential&nbsp;
nearby objects (which include satellites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:41.760
and space debris) and has the ability&nbsp;
to move out of the way far in advance.

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:47.720
Now, in addition to its Tiangong program, China&nbsp;
also has a lunar program, which hopes to land a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:55.320
taikonaut on the moon by around 2030. That’s&nbsp;
just a few years away. But it’s also possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.320 --> 00:09:00.640
that China is planning to surprise the world&nbsp;
by landing on the moon even sooner. That’s why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:06.440
there’s pressure on NASA to return humans to the&nbsp;
moon as soon as possible. As we saw in Episode 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:13.000
Artemis II sent four astronauts to the moon in&nbsp;
April of 2026 but they just circled the moon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:19.800
and did not land. It will be up to the crew&nbsp;
of Artemis IV to actually land on the moon—for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:26.280
the first time in over 50 years. That mission&nbsp;
is currently planned for 2028 but plans often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:34.440
get delayed so it truly is a race to see who will&nbsp;
land first: the Americans again or the Chinese?

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:39.120
Okay, let’s now go back and discuss&nbsp;
the other aspect of the new space race:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:44.120
the race between several private companies&nbsp;
to become NASA’s preferred partner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:49.320
This part is often referred to as the&nbsp;
“billionaires” space race, because, well, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:54.440
involves some of the richest people on the planet.&nbsp;
The first billionaire to help build a private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.440 --> 00:10:00.960
spacecraft was one of the founders of Microsoft.&nbsp;
Not Bill Gates, but the other guy: Paul Allen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:07.120
He funded the creation of SpaceShipOne, which&nbsp;
in 2004, became the first private spacecraft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:13.320
to fly above the 100 kilometre line (which&nbsp;
is the international definition of space).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:19.560
But it’s important to note that SpaceShipOne—as&nbsp;
well as its successor, SpaceShipTwo—are only able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:25.880
to make what’s called suborbital flights. As we&nbsp;
discussed in Episode 1, suborbital flights are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:32.720
not able to stay up in space very long (usually&nbsp;
just 5-10 minutes) because after this, they get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:37.920
pulled down by gravity. In order to *really* go&nbsp;
to space (meaning: to stay there for awhile),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:43.920
you need to build a spacecraft that can reach&nbsp;
Low Earth Orbit (which is far more difficult).

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:49.440
So that was the goal that four companies raced&nbsp;
towards in the hopes of building something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:56.920
could replace the space shuttle. These companies&nbsp;
were: SpaceX (owned by Elon Musk), Blue Origin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.920 --> 00:11:05.600
(owned by Jeff Bezos), Sierra Nevada (owned by&nbsp;
Fatih &amp; Eren Özmen, and Boeing (a public company).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:11.520
In the end, NASA decided to work with primarily&nbsp;
SpaceX (which had built the reusable Dragon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:18.680
capsule) as well as Boeing (which went on to build&nbsp;
the reusable Starliner capsule). Sierra Nevada&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:24.920
had built a reusable spaceplane called the Dream&nbsp;
Chaser, that looked a lot like the space shuttle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:31.360
but it was not chosen; and Blue Origin, well&nbsp;
it actually dropped out of the race and opted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:39.120
to create the reusable New Shepard capsule&nbsp;
instead (which, take note, is only suborbital).

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:46.280
By 2012, SpaceX’s Dragon started to make&nbsp;
cargo-only trips to the ISS but it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:53.760
take until 2020 before the first crewed Dragon&nbsp;
(called Dragon2) would fly to the station.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:59.360
After a successful demo flight in May (with&nbsp;
two passengers), it made its first official&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:05.440
ISS crew trip in November of that year,&nbsp;
delivering four astronauts to the ISS.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:13.080
Since then, it’s made 12 more ISS crew trips,&nbsp;
with the next one being planned for March of 2027.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:17.400
Now, just like with the space shuttle, there&nbsp;
are actually several versions of the Dragon2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:24.320
These are named Endeavour, Resilience,&nbsp;
Endurance, Freedom, and Grace—and so far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:31.520
all have been used by NASA. But SpaceX, being&nbsp;
a commercial venture, has also rented out its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:38.200
Dragon2 capsules to several private businesses&nbsp;
and private individuals. For example, in 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:44.880
a Dragon2 was used for the Inspiration4 flight,&nbsp;
funded by billionaire Jared Isaacman. This was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:50.560
the first ever fully private orbital spaceflight&nbsp;
and it took place as part of a fundraiser for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:57.240
children’s hospital. Several Dragon2’s have also&nbsp;
been used by Axiom Space, which arranges trips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.240 --> 00:13:04.320
to the ISS for both space tourists as well as for&nbsp;
foreign governments. Then there was Polaris Dawn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:11.760
(again, funded by Jared Isaacman) as well as&nbsp;
Fram2 (funded by crypto-billionaire Chun Wang).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:19.080
It’s notable in that, so far, it’s the only human&nbsp;
spaceflight that has flown in a polar orbit.

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:25.120
What about the Boeing Starliner though? Well,&nbsp;
it’s been less successful so far. It’s first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:31.600
crewed flight took place in June of 2024 and&nbsp;
while it made it safety to the space station,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:38.560
it was determined that it was not safe to use&nbsp;
for the journey back, which effectively stranded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:46.080
the astronauts on the ISS, until they were able to&nbsp;
get a ride home on a Dragon2. Now, as I mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:51.560
Blue Origin dropped out of NASA’s commercial&nbsp;
crew program. However, it went on to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:58.840
the New Shepard, which in 2021, became the first&nbsp;
private spaceflight to take passengers to space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.840 --> 00:14:06.480
(albeit only on a suborbital flight). It’s&nbsp;
fully automated and doesn’t require a pilot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:13.800
Aboard its very first flight was owner Jeff Bezos,&nbsp;
his brother Mark, Oliver Daemen (who, to date, is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:21.240
the youngest person to ever fly to space, at age&nbsp;
18), and a very special person named Wally Funk.

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:27.440
To explain who she is, I have to go all the&nbsp;
way back to the US’s very first space program:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:32.680
Project Mercury. In the late 1950s,&nbsp;
which is when the project began,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:38.760
no human had ever been to space and therefore the&nbsp;
first thing that NASA had to do was determine who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:45.760
were the best people for the job. Being the 1950s,&nbsp;
they of course, ended up choosing seven men—all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:52.760
of whom were military pilots. They became the&nbsp;
Mercury 7 and all seven ended up going to space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:58.800
However, at the same time, one of the main NASA&nbsp;
doctors decided to privately fund a project to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:04.800
see if women could also be suitable. So, he got&nbsp;
a group of women to go through the same rigorous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:10.800
testing that the men had to go through. And, what&nbsp;
do you know? 13 women passed all of the tests.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:17.840
They became known as the Mercury 13 but none of&nbsp;
them were ever selected by NASA to go to space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:25.000
Let’s now go back down to July 2021. By this&nbsp;
point, only one of the Mercury 13 women was still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:32.760
living and that was 82-year-old Wally Funk. So,&nbsp;
in the end, at least one of the Mercury 13 women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:39.320
did end up going to space, and at the time, she&nbsp;
also became the oldest person to ever to so.

00:15:39.320 --> 00:15:43.800
The second time Blue Origin’s New&nbsp;
Shepard sent humans into space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:49.440
they brought along yet another special&nbsp;
passenger. This time it was William Shatner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:58.760
aka Captain James T. Kirk from the original Star&nbsp;
Trek series. At the time, he was 90 years old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:05.600
and thus broke Wally Funk’s record. However,&nbsp;
as of 2026, he is no longer the oldest person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:11.280
to have gone to space. Because, in 2024,&nbsp;
his record was beat by another 90 year old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:18.680
who was about 2 months older. That person was Ed&nbsp;
Dwight, a Black American who, 63 years earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:24.440
had been selected for astronaut training but who&nbsp;
never did end up becoming an astronaut back then.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:30.200
To date, Blue Origin has launched 17&nbsp;
suborbital flights with passengers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.200 --> 00:16:35.760
including the controversial one last year&nbsp;
that included Justin Trudeau’s girlfriend.

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:42.640
Okay, so that pretty much take us up to date in&nbsp;
terms of human spaceflight in Low Earth Orbit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:48.960
So, what about the future? What new developments&nbsp;
can we expect to see in the next 5-10 years?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:55.840
Well, for one thing, having housed 75 permanent&nbsp;
expeditions over 26 years, the International&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.840 --> 00:17:02.600
Space Station is nearing the end of its life&nbsp;
and is likely to be decommissioned in 2030.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:08.640
NASA has no plans to build a replacement and&nbsp;
instead will be focusing on human missions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:14.400
to the Moon and eventually, Mars. Which means&nbsp;
that what we’re likely to see in the near future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:19.760
is a bunch of smaller, commercially-operated&nbsp;
space stations in Low Earth Orbit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:27.360
Two that are already in the works include Haven-1&nbsp;
by a company called Vast, and the Axiom Station,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:34.480
run by Axiom. That one’s interesting in that the&nbsp;
plan is to attach the first module to the ISS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.480 --> 00:17:41.520
but then later, to detach it and make it the&nbsp;
first section of a new space station instead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:47.640
Russia is considering doing something similar,&nbsp;
meaning that it might add a new module to the ISS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:55.440
but then later detach it to start building a&nbsp;
new, Russian-only station. So, by say, 2033,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.440 --> 00:18:02.120
we might have a Chinese station, a Russian&nbsp;
station, and several private US ones.

00:18:02.120 --> 00:18:07.880
The other thing to note is that,&nbsp;
by 2028, we’ll probably see India&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.880 --> 00:18:14.960
become the fourth country with the capability&nbsp;
of sending humans to space, via its Gaganyaan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:22.080
program. At that point, astronauts, cosmonauts,&nbsp;
and taikonauts will be joined by Vyomanauts.

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:27.800
Okay, so that was a look at the new,&nbsp;
current space race. Like I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:33.320
in the fourth and final episode of this series,&nbsp;
we’ll be looking at missions to other planets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:39.000
using robotic spacecraft. So keep an&nbsp;
eye out for that. And don’t forget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:45.040
to check out UsefulCharts.com if you’re&nbsp;
interested in getting a copy of this poster.

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:46.120
Thanks for watching.

